// The ShapeType of the classic ShapesDemo GUIs.
//
// THERE ARE TWO ShapeTypes UNDER THIS NAME, and this is one of them.
// The other is `omg-dds-rtps.idl` beside this file: `@appendable`, five
// members. Read `README.md` here before you use either.
//
// Who speaks this one
//   The interactive ShapesDemo applications — RTI Connext's (started
//   `-dataType Shape`), CycloneDDS's, Fast DDS's — on the `Square` /
//   `Circle` / `Triangle` topics, and the four-stack example suite
//   `zerodds-examples/cross-vendor-shapes/`, whose `ShapeType.idl` is
//   this same declaration. That copy is the one the examples build
//   against; this one is the in-tree copy the agreement test reads, and
//   the two must stay identical in substance.
//
// Why `@final` and not `@appendable`
//   Because that is what the GUIs' own type support was generated
//   from, and it is the only extensibility that interoperates with a
//   peer that does not negotiate XTypes. CycloneDDS's IDL compiler also
//   defaults to `@final`; Fast DDS's `topic_instances` example chose
//   `@appendable`, which is why the example suite regenerates it.
//   XTypes 1.3 Tab. 59: `@final` is PLAIN_CDR2 under XCDR2 — no
//   DHEADER, encapsulation id `CDR2_LE` (`0x00 0x07`).
//
// What checks it
//   `crates/dcps/tests/shapes_type_idl_agreement.rs`, which asserts
//   that this form and the OMG form differ on the wire in every mode —
//   the two are not interchangeable and nothing may treat them as one.

@extensibility(FINAL)
struct ShapeType {
    @key string color;
    long x;
    long y;
    long shapesize;
};
